# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ServiceTitan API credentials.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your values. `.env` is gitignored and
# must NEVER be committed or shared - these credentials can read your entire
# ServiceTitan account.
#
# HOW THE NAMES WORK
#
#   SERVICETITAN_APP_KEY              shared - used by every location
#   SERVICETITAN_APP_KEY_SPRINGFIELD  used only by the location whose "env" in
#                                     config.json is SPRINGFIELD
#
# Every one of the four credentials accepts both forms. A location's own key
# wins; the unsuffixed key is the fallback for any location that does not set
# its own. Which means:
#
#   ONE LOCATION   Fill in the four unsuffixed keys below, delete the per-
#                  location section, and leave "env" out of config.json.
#
#   MANY LOCATIONS Set the app key once, unsuffixed - it belongs to your app,
#                  not to any one tenant, so it is the same everywhere. Then
#                  give each location its own suffixed client id, client secret
#                  and tenant id, since those really do differ per tenant.
#
# WHERE THESE COME FROM, in the ServiceTitan developer portal
# (https://developer.servicetitan.io - sign in with your ServiceTitan account):
#
#   1. Create an app, or open an existing one.
#   2. Client ID + Client Secret come from the app's credentials. The secret is
#      shown ONCE at creation - if you lose it you must generate a new one.
#   3. App Key is on the app page, and is the same for every tenant your app is
#      connected to. That is why it is the unsuffixed one below.
#   4. Tenant ID is per ServiceTitan account. Find it in Settings > Integrations,
#      or in the URL when you are signed in to that tenant.
#   5. Grant the app READ scopes for: Job Planning & Management, Accounting,
#      Sales & Estimates, Marketing, Telecom, Inventory, Memberships, CRM,
#      Dispatch and Settings. The dashboard only ever issues GET requests, so
#      read-only scopes are sufficient and are what you should grant.
#
# Then run:  servicetitan auth check
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Shared by every location ----------------------------------------------

SERVICETITAN_APP_KEY=

# A single-location setup fills in these three as well, then deletes everything
# below. Leave them blank if every location has its own suffixed keys.
SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_ID=
SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_SECRET=
SERVICETITAN_TENANT_ID=


# --- Per location ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Delete this whole section if you have one location. Otherwise one block per
# location, the suffix matching that location's "env" in config.json. The two
# blocks below match the two locations in config.example.json.
#
# Do not leave a tenant id blank here and rely on the shared one above: two
# locations pointing at the same tenant pull the same data twice. The scripts
# refuse to run if that happens, but it is worth not writing in the first place.

SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_ID_SPRINGFIELD=
SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_SECRET_SPRINGFIELD=
SERVICETITAN_TENANT_ID_SPRINGFIELD=

SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_ID_LOCATION_2=
SERVICETITAN_CLIENT_SECRET_LOCATION_2=
SERVICETITAN_TENANT_ID_LOCATION_2=
